    "content": "<div style='display:flex;gap:.5rem;margin-bottom:.5rem'><span class='tag'>ISO/IEC</span> <span class='tag'>2023</span></div>\n<h1>Government / Public Sector AI Compliance: ISO 42001</h1>\n<p class='lead'>ISO/IEC 42001 offers public-sector organisations a management-system framework for AI governance that aligns with existing public-administration quality and risk standards. It supports transparency, accountability, and citizen trust in government AI.</p>\n\n<div class='meta-bar'>\n  <span class='tag'><strong>Sector:</strong> Government / Public Sector</span>\n  <span class='tag'><strong>Regulation:</strong> ISO 42001</span>\n  <span class='tag'><strong>CSOAI Articles:</strong> 4 mapped</span>\n</div>\n\n<h2>Key Compliance Requirements</h2>\n<div class='requirement-box'><h3>Scope & context for public-sector AI</h3><p>Clause 4 requires mapping AI use cases across citizen services, law enforcement, infrastructure, and policy analysis, identifying public-interest and legal requirements.</p></div>\n<div class='requirement-box'><h3>Leadership & AI policy</h3><p>Clause 5 mandates that senior public leadership owns AI policy, covering transparency, non-discrimination, administrative-law compliance, and citizen redress.</p></div>\n<div class='requirement-box'><h3>AI risk assessment for public services</h3><p>Clause 6 requires documented risk assessment for AI affecting citizen rights — covering bias, error, privacy, security, and democratic legitimacy.</p></div>\n<div class='requirement-box'><h3>Performance evaluation & public accountability</h3><p>Clause 9 mandates monitoring of AI outcomes against public-service KPIs, with public reporting and feedback mechanisms for citizens and oversight bodies.</p></div>\n\n<h2>CSOAI Charter Articles</h2>\n<ul class='articles'>\n<li>Article 13 — Risk Management</li>\n<li>Article 29 — Partnership Charter</li>\n<li>Article 33 — Transparency Obligations</li>\n<li>Article 47 — Audit Right</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>Relevant MCPs</h2>\n<p style='color:var(--muted);font-size:.9rem'>These MCP servers operationalise compliance for this sector and regulation:</p>\n<span class='mcp-tag'>iso-42001-gap-analyzer-mcp</span> <span class='mcp-tag'>iso-42001-documentation-generator-mcp</span> <span class='mcp-tag'>csoai-rms</span> \n\n<h2>Framework Reference</h2>\n<p>See the full <a href='/frameworks/iso-42001'>ISO 42001 crosswalk</a> for the published clause-by-clause mappings. The signed crosswalk covers four regimes; anything outside them is described, not crosswalked.</p>\n\n<div class='cta'>\n  <h2>Run the free risk classifier</h2>\n  <p style='color:#e6f7ec;margin:.5rem 0'>The free self-serve tool is an EU AI Act risk classifier: describe the system in text and a deterministic decision table returns the Annex III tier and the gaps against a fixed Art 9–15/50 control set. It covers one regulation, it never contacts your system, and it is not a bench run. A GSPC bench run against a frozen bank is arranged directly and is not yet self-serve.</p>\n  <a href='/certify'>Get Measured &rarr;</a>\n  <a href='/asti'>See AI Self-State Index</a>\n  <a href='/frameworks'>All Frameworks</a>\n</div>\n\n<p class='foot'>&copy; 2026 CSOAI LTD &middot; UK Companies House <strong>16939677</strong><br>\nSector crosswalk &middot; CC BY 4.0 &middot; <a href='/methodology'>Methodology</a> &middot; <a href='mailto:nicholas@csoai.org'>Contact</a><br>\n<em>\"The ISO for AI safety.\"</em></p>",
