    "content": "<div style='display:flex;gap:.5rem;margin-bottom:.5rem'><span class='tag'>United Kingdom</span> <span class='tag'>2024</span></div>\n<h1>Non-profit / NGO AI Compliance: UK AI Regulation</h1>\n<p class='lead'>UK non-profit AI is overseen by the Charity Commission for charitable organisations, the ICO for data protection, and sector regulators for specific activities. The pro-innovation approach means AI governance must align with existing charitable-law and fiduciary obligations.</p>\n\n<div class='meta-bar'>\n  <span class='tag'><strong>Sector:</strong> Non-profit / NGO</span>\n  <span class='tag'><strong>Regulation:</strong> UK AI Regulation</span>\n  <span class='tag'><strong>CSOAI Articles:</strong> 4 mapped</span>\n</div>\n\n<h2>Key Compliance Requirements</h2>\n<div class='requirement-box'><h3>Charity Commission guidance on AI</h3><p>The Charity Commission expects charities using AI to ensure it supports charitable purposes, does not harm beneficiaries, and maintains public trust and confidence.</p></div>\n<div class='requirement-box'><h3>Fiduciary duties & AI governance</h3><p>Charity trustees remain personally responsible for AI-driven decisions affecting charitable operations, beneficiary welfare, and organisational reputation.</p></div>\n<div class='requirement-box'><h3>Fundraising Regulator expectations</h3><p>Charities using AI for donor targeting and fundraising must comply with the Code of Fundraising Practice, ensuring transparency and respecting donor preferences.</p></div>\n<div class='requirement-box'><h3>ICO guidance on beneficiary data & AI</h3><p>The ICO expects DPIAs for high-risk processing of beneficiary and donor data, with transparency and data-minimisation in AI-driven analytics.</p></div>\n\n<h2>CSOAI Charter Articles</h2>\n<ul class='articles'>\n<li>Article 29 — Partnership Charter</li>\n<li>Article 33 — Transparency Obligations</li>\n<li>Article 47 — Audit Right</li>\n<li>Article 13 — Risk Management</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>Relevant MCPs</h2>\n<p style='color:var(--muted);font-size:.9rem'>These MCP servers operationalise compliance for this sector and regulation:</p>\n<span class='mcp-tag'>csoai-uk-ai-reg</span> <span class='mcp-tag'>csoai-deployer-pack</span> <span class='mcp-tag'>csoai-rms</span> \n\n<h2>Framework Reference</h2>\n<p>See the full <a href='/frameworks/uk-ai-regulation'>UK AI Regulation crosswalk</a> for the published clause-by-clause mappings. The signed crosswalk covers four regimes; anything outside them is described, not crosswalked.</p>\n\n<div class='cta'>\n  <h2>Run the free risk classifier</h2>\n  <p style='color:#e6f7ec;margin:.5rem 0'>The free self-serve tool is an EU AI Act risk classifier: describe the system in text and a deterministic decision table returns the Annex III tier and the gaps against a fixed Art 9–15/50 control set. It covers one regulation, it never contacts your system, and it is not a bench run. A GSPC bench run against a frozen bank is arranged directly and is not yet self-serve.</p>\n  <a href='/certify'>Get Measured &rarr;</a>\n  <a href='/asti'>See AI Self-State Index</a>\n  <a href='/frameworks'>All Frameworks</a>\n</div>\n\n<p class='foot'>&copy; 2026 CSOAI LTD &middot; UK Companies House <strong>16939677</strong><br>\nSector crosswalk &middot; CC BY 4.0 &middot; <a href='/methodology'>Methodology</a> &middot; <a href='mailto:nicholas@csoai.org'>Contact</a><br>\n<em>\"The ISO for AI safety.\"</em></p>",
