    "content": "<div style='display:flex;gap:.5rem;margin-bottom:.5rem'><span class='tag'>European Union</span> <span class='tag'>2016</span></div>\n<h1>Transportation / Logistics AI Compliance: GDPR</h1>\n<p class='lead'>Transportation AI processes location data, driver behaviour profiles, passenger biometric data, and shipment information. Location data is personal data under GDPR, and real-time tracking triggers heightened sensitivity around surveillance and automated decision-making.</p>\n\n<div class='meta-bar'>\n  <span class='tag'><strong>Sector:</strong> Transportation / Logistics</span>\n  <span class='tag'><strong>Regulation:</strong> GDPR</span>\n  <span class='tag'><strong>CSOAI Articles:</strong> 4 mapped</span>\n</div>\n\n<h2>Key Compliance Requirements</h2>\n<div class='requirement-box'><h3>Location data as personal data</h3><p>GPS tracking, route history, and geofencing data are personal data under GDPR; real-time driver tracking requires clear legal basis and transparency.</p></div>\n<div class='requirement-box'><h3>Profiling & automated decisions for drivers</h3><p>AI-driven driver scoring, performance management, and assignment algorithms trigger Article 22 rights, requiring human review for adverse decisions.</p></div>\n<div class='requirement-box'><h3>Data minimisation in fleet analytics</h3><p>Fleet-management AI must collect only the driver and vehicle data necessary for the specific operational purpose, with clear retention and deletion schedules.</p></div>\n<div class='requirement-box'><h3>Cross-border data flows for global logistics</h3><p>Multinational logistics operators must ensure lawful cross-border transfers of driver, passenger, and shipment data to centralised AI platforms.</p></div>\n\n<h2>CSOAI Charter Articles</h2>\n<ul class='articles'>\n<li>Article 43 — Sovereignty Principle</li>\n<li>Article 33 — Transparency Obligations</li>\n<li>Article 14 — Data Governance</li>\n<li>Article 47 — Audit Right</li>\n</ul>\n\n<h2>Relevant MCPs</h2>\n<p style='color:var(--muted);font-size:.9rem'>These MCP servers operationalise compliance for this sector and regulation:</p>\n<span class='mcp-tag'>gdpr-article-checker-mcp</span> <span class='mcp-tag'>gdpr-dpia-generator-mcp</span> <span class='mcp-tag'>csoai-data-governance</span> \n\n<h2>Framework Reference</h2>\n<p>See the full <a href='/frameworks/gdpr'>GDPR crosswalk</a> for the published clause-by-clause mappings. The signed crosswalk covers four regimes; anything outside them is described, not crosswalked.</p>\n\n<div class='cta'>\n  <h2>Run the free risk classifier</h2>\n  <p style='color:#e6f7ec;margin:.5rem 0'>The free self-serve tool is an EU AI Act risk classifier: describe the system in text and a deterministic decision table returns the Annex III tier and the gaps against a fixed Art 9–15/50 control set. It covers one regulation, it never contacts your system, and it is not a bench run. A GSPC bench run against a frozen bank is arranged directly and is not yet self-serve.</p>\n  <a href='/certify'>Get Measured &rarr;</a>\n  <a href='/asti'>See AI Self-State Index</a>\n  <a href='/frameworks'>All Frameworks</a>\n</div>\n\n<p class='foot'>&copy; 2026 CSOAI LTD &middot; UK Companies House <strong>16939677</strong><br>\nSector crosswalk &middot; CC BY 4.0 &middot; <a href='/methodology'>Methodology</a> &middot; <a href='mailto:nicholas@csoai.org'>Contact</a><br>\n<em>\"The ISO for AI safety.\"</em></p>",
